      <thead>
        <tr><th>Laptop</th><th>Buyback likelihood</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {acceptedTable.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{row.laptop}</td>
            <td>{row.buyback_likelihood}</td>
            <td>{row.notes}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How the process works</h2>
    <ol class="how-to-list">
      {howToSteps.map((s) => (
        <li><strong>{s.name}.</strong> {s.text}</li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How the buyback estimate is actually calculated</h2>
    <p>The number is based on second-hand market data for that specific model plus condition adjustments. Five variables move it:</p>
    <p>Brand — Apple retains value best; ThinkPad, Dell business, HP EliteBook next; consumer brands lower; no-brand laptops usually recycling only.</p>
    <p>Model + year — a 2022 XPS 13 is not the same product as a 2015 XPS 13. Year matters significantly for the resale market.</p>
    <p>Condition — powers on cleanly, screen intact, keys working, casing OK, battery holds reasonable charge.</p>
    <p>Drive — laptop with SSD/HDD included is worth more than one without.</p>
    <p>Cosmetics — dents, scratches, worn keyboard, faded screen all reduce the number.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Estimate vs confirmed quote — and declining</h2>
    <p>Every quote before physical inspection is an estimate, labelled that way in the message. Physical inspection almost always reveals something the photos didn't:</p>
    <p>Keys that stick, a battery that drains in an hour, a hinge that grinds, a subtle screen defect, a fan that never quiets down — small things that materially affect resale value.</p>
    <p>Sometimes inspection reveals better condition than the photos suggested — the quote goes up.</p>
    <p>If the confirmed quote comes in lower than the estimate and you'd rather not sell at that number, you can decline. The team doesn't collect the laptop, no pickup fee, no restocking fee, no consequence. The laptop stays with you.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Data on the laptop</h2>
    <p>For clean buyback, factory-reset the laptop before pickup: back up what you want, sign out of iCloud/Apple ID (macOS) or Microsoft account (Windows), then factory reset. This makes the transaction cleaner and removes any residual data risk to you.</p>
    <p>If you can't (forgotten password, laptop won't boot, admin lock), flag it when booking. The laptop routes through data destruction — the drive is wiped or physically shredded before the laptop enters any resale flow. Certificate of Destruction available on request. See /data-destruction/ for the full workflow.</p>
  </section>

